Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Own UEFI boot-disk builder - image.zero

The builder is written entirely in Zero:
build/zero --host < image.zero > build/image
chmod +x build/image
It writes FAT16 metadata itself; no external formatter or runtime constructs
this filesystem. The same FAT image offers AMD64 and IA32 UEFI fallback
loaders. BIOS optical boot uses the separate BIOS entry described in ISO.ztext.
Its .img output is an internal build intermediate embedded as the ISO's
El Torito UEFI boot payload. It is not separately distributed. The current
release supplies only the genuine ISO9660/El Torito optical image; no raw
or VDI hard-disk images are bundled or relabelled as ISO files.

Input contract

Concatenate exactly five fixed-size slots, already padded with zero bytes:

    65536 bytes   AMD64 PE32+ EFI loader (BOOTX64.EFI)
    65536 bytes   IA32 PE32 EFI loader (BOOTIA32.EFI)
    491520 bytes  x86-64 raw kernel (KERNEL.BIN)
    491520 bytes  x86 raw kernel (KERNEL32.BIN)
    1048576 bytes native compiler bundle (TOOLS.BIN), generated by bundle.zero

The complete input is 2162688 bytes. Reject oversized actual binaries before
padding them; a file utility must never truncate an oversized program to fit.
The normal build.sh prepares these slots and invokes the writer. UEFI opens
the padded files at their declared sizes; trailing padding is not executable
code and the loader's explicit kernel allocation/read bounds cover the slot.

The writer streams one 512-byte sector at a time, reserving only 512 bytes.
It does not buffer all five payloads. At the first sector of each EFI slot it
checks MZ/PE, the expected machine and optional-header magic, two sections,
standard 512/4096 file/section alignment, 512-byte headers, EFI application
subsystem, ordered nonempty raw sections and a final section end<= 65536.
These checks target the PE layouts emitted by our own compiler, not every
possible third-party PE file. The raw kernel bytes are opaque to this tool. TOOLS.BIN must start with
ZRBNDL01, version1, size1048576 and app count6; bundle.zero validates its
individual app headers and padding before creating it.

Partial reads/writes are handled explicitly and EINTR is retried. Metadata
is emitted before all input is available, so a later failure can leave a
partial image. Only use output after successful exit. Excess input is checked
immediately after all five slots, before the trailing unused disk area.

Output layout

The result is a reproducible 16777216-byte FAT16 superfloppy, with 512-byte
sectors and one sector per cluster:

    sector 0       BPB, extended BPB and 55AA signature; no BIOS boot code
    sectors 1..128 first FAT
    129..256      second identical FAT
    257..288      fixed 512-entry root directory
    289           cluster 2: EFI directory
    290           cluster 3: EFI/BOOT directory
    291..418      clusters 4..131: EFI/BOOT/BOOTX64.EFI, 65536 bytes
    419..546      clusters 132..259: EFI/BOOT/BOOTIA32.EFI, 65536 bytes
    547..1506     clusters260..1219: KERNEL.BIN, 491520 bytes
    1507..2466    clusters1220..2179: KERNEL32.BIN, 491520 bytes
    2467..4514    clusters2180..4227: TOOLS.BIN, 1048576 bytes
    4515..32767   unused zero-filled disk area

File chains can cross FAT sectors. Each file's last cluster is FFFF; clusters
outside the five files and the two directories remain free. Root contains
EFI, both kernels, TOOLS.BIN and the volume label ZERO SYSTEM. Directory timestamps are
1980-01-01 at 00:00. Both FAT copies are generated from the same cluster rules.
All input payload bytes, including slot padding, are copied unchanged.

Persistent user files belong on a separate user-created or existing writable
disk. This internal FAT image is boot media and never creates or replaces a
user data disk. The distributed ISO embeds it read-only for UEFI. See
VIRTUALBOX.ztext for creating a blank virtual disk and explicit ZeroFS setup.

Exit statuses
0 success; 1 input read error; 2 short input or invalid EFI/bundle header;
3 excess input; 4 output write error. Host SIGPIPE behavior still applies.

Historical writer verification (before the384KiB slot growth)
Synthetic paired EFI slots, including a deliberately non-executable IA32
header fixture, were used to isolate format/streaming tests from CPU boot.
The writer produced exactly 16 MiB; both FAT copies and every extracted payload
matched. fsck.fat -n reported a valid filesystem and mtools listed both UEFI
fallback files and extracted EFI32/kernel32 bytes exactly. These external
utilities only inspected the result; they did not construct it.
Short/excess input, invalid PE and /dev/full output produced the expected
statuses. 97-byte fragmented input reproduced identical output. Final actual
firmware boots are recorded separately in verification.ztext by integration.

An additional OVMF/QEMU boot used the real prior AMD64 loader/kernel in their
new padded slots and the synthetic IA32 fixture in the unused fallback slot.
It traversed the new FAT chains, read the complete 128 KiB kernel file, left
boot services and reached ZeroFS/terminal. This validates the new FAT layout
for the AMD64 path; it is not an IA32 firmware-boot test. The test used a
snapshot data disk, and the original data.img checksum stayed unchanged.

Historical384KiB layout checks
See verification/boot-growth-0.7.0/results.ztext. The final-slot probe boots in
both OVMF architectures and verifies markers across both128KiB boundaries
and byte393215, after ExitBootServices. The FAT image remains16MiB and both
kernel chains contain768 clusters. Independent fsck.fat/mtools inspection,
payload roundtrips and streaming failure tests validate this enlarged layout.

The historical 448 KiB capacity-only probes remain in
verification/boot-capacity-448k/. Current 480 KiB slots and the native compiler
bundle are checked in verification/guest-bundle-480k/; the probe verifies the
whole 1 MiB loaded bundle, every 8 MiB workspace page and the final kernel
byte on BIOS/UEFI, both 32- and 64-bit. Native compiler execution evidence is
separate in verification/guest-app/bundle/. Final OS integration results
remain separate in verification.ztext.
